Publish this repo as static files — do not run Jekyll over it.

GitHub Pages runs Jekyll by default. This site has no use for it: there is no
_config.yml, no _layouts, no _includes, and not one file carries YAML front
matter. Jekyll's only effect was to walk all 14,842 tracked files (~13,700 of
them images under img/) and copy them out unchanged.

That cost real time, and it grew until it broke the deploy:

    604fbba1   341,958 ms   built
    aa7ee02c   391,390 ms   built
    c577ca3f   398,616 ms   built
    c577ca3f   412,003 ms   built     <- 6m52s, last one to make it
    0f936d8d        errored           <- "Page build failed"

Pages caps a build at 10 minutes. The trend was going one way, so the failure
was going to happen on whichever commit crossed the line — it had nothing to do
with the contents of that commit.

With this file present Pages skips Jekyll and publishes the tree directly, so
a deploy is a file copy and the timeout stops being reachable.

Related, and NOT fixed here: img/ is still tracked even though the images now
serve from the R2 CDN (js/core/asset-config.js). Untracking them would remove
~13,700 files from the repo, but it needs a history rewrite and a force-push —
see tools/purge-img-history.sh, which is deliberately still waiting.
